{:edb/db-before-t 884 :edb/db-after-t 885 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534197 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:38:26.300Z" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186147427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413744 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186147718 1050 900.0 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186148743 1064 "Suppose we are given the planes ${\\Pi_{{1}}}: 2x -y+z=0$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}: -x + y-z=1.$ First, notice that:\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,\\:-1,\\: 1 \\rangle.$\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -1,\\: 1,\\: -1 \\rangle.$\n\nIf two distinct planes intersect each other, their intersection will always be a straight line. A direction vector $\\mathbf{v}$ for that line can be found by computing the cross product of the normal vectors of the two planes. \n\n![](images/0c/0cf233086eccae47e640d3599908572e2c3a9996dd84d861e4d659c03108105c.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n![](images/93/93e830fa9649586d61442813a6afb04ba2f48b4b035171ce65f17dd5debdb4d5.svg)" 13194139534197 17592186045417 false] [17592186148743 1064 "Suppose we are given the planes ${\\Pi_{{1}}}: 2x -y+z=0$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}: -x + y-z=1.$ First, notice that:\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,\\:-1,\\: 1 \\rangle.$\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -1,\\: 1,\\: -1 \\rangle.$\n\nIf two distinct planes intersect each other, their intersection will always be a straight line. A direction vector $\\mathbf{v}$ for that line can be found by computing the cross product of the normal vectors of the two planes. \n\n![](images/0c/0cf233086eccae47e640d3599908572e2c3a9996dd84d861e4d659c03108105c.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186148745 1064 "Consider a line that is given by its vector equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $y=2$ at every point on the line, the corresponding Cartesian equations of this line are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1, \\quad y=2.\n$$\n\nWe may therefore interpret this line as the intersection of the planes $\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1$ and $y=2$.\n\nLet's look at one more example. Given the line \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwe can write its Cartesian equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-2}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}2 = \\dfrac{z-1}3\n$$\n\nwhich can be further written as the system of two equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{-5}=\\frac{y-1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, again, our line can be represented as the intersection of the planes $2x+5y-9=0$ and $3y-2z-1=0$." 13194139534197 17592186045417 false] [17592186148745 1064 "Consider a line that is given by its vector equation\n![](images/ca/ca43fd13bd65c544d66a4de2801871c2a79087fd960db44696ce6068d30c8377.svg)\n\nSince $y=2$ at every point on the line, the corresponding Cartesian equations of this line are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1, \\quad y=2.\n$$\n\nWe may therefore interpret this line as the intersection of the planes $\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1$ and $y=2$.\n\nLet's look at one more example. Given the line \n\n![](images/1a/1a97b5bcff75bc7306d14a8af3fb1f169a2e7bf865be2e7a9c6d2741eecc63a6.svg)\n\nwe can write its Cartesian equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-2}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}2 = \\dfrac{z-1}3\n$$\n\nwhich can be further written as the system of two equations:\n\n![](images/1f/1f4cce850809f3fda876dd96e1cf9c65fd873df97bbba44e291b611896c86ca4.svg)\n\nSo, again, our line can be represented as the intersection of the planes $2x+5y-9=0$ and $3y-2z-1=0$." 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413738 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413738 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413738 1083 #uuid "0adea858-c23c-564c-9506-9e4a60396c0c" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413739 1080 "I and III only" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413739 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413739 1083 #uuid "22b5f065-43d8-5efe-9322-eca1974cc3d0" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413740 1080 "III only" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413740 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413740 1083 #uuid "6d9614ef-645c-5eb3-83fc-f33c2f35aca1" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413741 1080 "II only" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413741 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413741 1083 #uuid "6dc4adca-656f-5e68-9890-f48be898ac6a" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413742 1080 "I only" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413742 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413742 1083 #uuid "f5604891-08a1-55fe-b657-ffce04ef2384" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413743 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413738 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413743 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413739 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413743 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413740 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413743 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413741 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413743 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413742 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413743 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413743 1077 "selection" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413743 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413741 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413743 1079 #uuid "aef76bea-df64-529b-85e7-fa51fb7e165e" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413744 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413743 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413744 1069 "q-71465" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413744 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413744 1071 "Given the two planes $x+y+z=0$ and $x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413744 1072 #uuid "abe7bafd-4069-5c23-9e34-e0cbd57e3732" 13194139534197 17592186045417 true] [17592186413744 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x+y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\ne ⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139534197 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0adea858-c23c-564c-9506-9e4a60396c0c" 17592186413738 "answer-22b5f065-43d8-5efe-9322-eca1974cc3d0" 17592186413739 "answer-6d9614ef-645c-5eb3-83fc-f33c2f35aca1" 17592186413740 "answer-6dc4adca-656f-5e68-9890-f48be898ac6a" 17592186413741 "answer-f5604891-08a1-55fe-b657-ffce04ef2384" 17592186413742 "answer-field-aef76bea-df64-529b-85e7-fa51fb7e165e" 17592186413743 "question-abe7bafd-4069-5c23-9e34-e0cbd57e3732" 17592186413744} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
